---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const circleCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'sqrt(r^2 - x^2)', label: 'upper semicircle', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '-sqrt(r^2 - x^2)', label: 'lower semicircle', color: '#7c3aed' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'r', label: 'radius r', min: 1, max: 5, step: 0.5, value: 3 },
  ],
  x: { min: -6, max: 6, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: -6, max: 6 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/circles-and-coordinate-geometry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Descartes' move (1637) was to glue algebra onto geometry: every point is a pair of numbers,
    every curve is an <strong>equation</strong>. Suddenly geometric questions become algebraic
    manipulations and vice versa. The circle is the cleanest example: its equation is just the
    Pythagorean theorem applied to the distance from the centre.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="x² + y² = r² drawn as two square-root halves — drag the radius" widget="plotter" config={circleCfg} />

  <p>
    The graph needs two curve pieces because a function can only return one y per x — a circle
    is <em>not</em> a function. That small annoyance teaches something real: functions are a
    discipline imposed on relations, and some shapes (circles, sideways parabolas) refuse it.
  </p>

  <h2>The coordinate toolkit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distance formula</strong> — Pythagoras again: d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²). Every conic section equation is a distance condition wearing algebra.</li>
    <li><strong>Midpoint and gradient</strong> — averages and slopes, the same two numbers from the linear-functions lesson.</li>
    <li><strong>General circle:</strong> (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r², centre (a, b). Expanding it gives x² + y² + Dx + Ey + F = 0 — completing the square (the quadratics lesson) recovers the centre and radius.</li>
    <li><strong>The other conics</strong> come from slicing cones: parabola, ellipse, hyperbola — each a distance condition, each appearing later in physics (orbits are ellipses, as Kepler's lesson shows).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Algebra and Trigonometry (Analytic Geometry); Descartes, La Géométrie (1637).</p>
</TopicPage>
